Szymon,

thanks for your patience, and for the very direct note on Friday, which was fair. I have now been through the logger export, the Tidewalk event history and the shift notes with Jarek, so what follows is what we can stand behind today. Where I say "about" it is because we are still reconciling two clocks (see point 4). I'm answering inline below, in the order you asked, so please excuse the formatting.

> 1. When exactly did the product go out of range, and what was the temperature?

The KC-Tag 4 in container KRVU 482917-3 (the one on your trailer OD-231) logged the first reading above the 5.5 C top of your permitted band at 22:05 on Thursday 13 June. The setpoint was +3.0 C and the box had been sitting at +2.9 to +3.2 all evening, so something changed suddenly, it was not a slow drift. By 23:30 the return-air reading was 11.4 C and it peaked at 13.8 C at 03:40 on the 14th. The tag logs every 5 minutes. The reading went back inside the band at 07:10, so I count 9 hours 5 minutes outside it. Rounded, the "nine hours" in Jarek's first message is right.

> 2. Why did nobody see it? Your own system is supposed to scream.

Two things happened together and I am not going to dress it up. The gateway in the yard that this tag reports through (hub OD-H02) had been rebooting in a loop since about 19:00, so the tag's readings were being buffered and only came through in lumps. The alarm that Tidewalk did generate went out once, to the single contact on file for your account, at 22:41, and that was the night dispatcher's desk phone queue, not a mobile. It was acknowledged on screen at 22:47 by a junior dispatcher (I am not naming them, that is for us to handle internally) and then nothing was done with it for a long time. The escalation to the shift lead should have fired after 20 minutes of no clearing and it did not, because the rule was attached to the alarm group "dairy-cold" and OD-231 was still in the old group "dairy-chilled". That is our configuration mistake and we have fixed it for every dairy account as of Saturday.

> 3. Who decided what, and when?

Olek Maraszek (senior dispatcher, nights) came on at 23:00 as usual, was told by the junior that "there was a dairy alarm, acked", and looked at the dashboard at 00:20. He saw the trailer at 11.9 C, tried the hub remotely, found it offline, and at 00:35 sent a driver, Radosław Pietrzak, to the yard to look at the physical unit. Radosław got there at 01:25. The reefer unit was running but the compressor was cycling off; he moved the setpoint by hand, and then at 01:50 decided together with Olek to switch the unit over to its back-up controller. That bought nothing for about two hours. It was Olek, not the junior, who rang Jarek at 04:10 and Olek who rang your transport desk at 05:20 to say the load was being moved (you were not in, the message was taken by a Ms Wrenfield); Jarek authorised the move itself.

At 05:55 the load was transferred into trailer OD-188, which was pre-cooled to 1.5 C. The tag travelled with the pallets and read back inside the band at 07:10 in the product itself. We did not release anything to the consignee at Hull without your say-so.

> 4. Your timestamps do not match mine.

No, they don't, and I know why. The KC-Hub clock at OD-H02 was running about 12 minutes slow after the reboot loop, which is exactly the gap between my 22:05 and your 21:53. Our figures above are corrected for that, yours are probably from the PDF export which is not. If you send me the PDF I will mark up which lines are shifted.

> 5. What are you going to do about the credit? Your account manager mentioned something on the phone.

Yusuf spoke to you on the 15th. I can confirm what he said and I have cleared it with Priya: Korvane will credit 14,500 EUR against your June invoice, no strings and no admission. It is on top of, not instead of, the service credit the MSA gives for an excursion of this length, which finance calculates separately from the schedule once the Excursion Hours are agreed between us. Yusuf will send the note.

Kind regards,
Benedikta

